 ### [Adaptive Delaunay Update](4923-adaptive-delaunay-update.md)
-Adaptive Delaunay Update is a strategy for maintaining the topological validity of a tetrahedral mesh during optimization-based volumetric reconstruction, specifically within the context of Radiance Meshes. It addresses the problem of computational cost versus geometric accuracy when vertex positions change during training. Unlike methods that recompute the Delaunay triangulation from scratch at every iteration, this method updates the topology only periodically, balancing GPU acceleration speed with rendering accuracy. It builds on [Tetrahedral representation and traversal](4396-tetrahedral-representation-and-traversal.md) by ensuring the underlying mesh remains a valid Delaunay triangulation despite vertex perturbations.
+Adaptive Delaunay Update is a technique used in differentiable volumetric reconstruction to maintain a valid Delaunay tetrahedralization of a radiance field during optimization. It addresses the problem of computational cost by decoupling the expensive topological recomputation from the frequent attribute updates. Unlike methods that recompute the Delaunay triangulation from scratch at every iteration, this method updates vertex positions and topology at a fixed, lower frequency, balancing the speed of GPU-accelerated rendering with the accuracy of the geometric representation.
 
 ### [GPU-based Projected Tetrahedra](7484-gpu-based-projected-tetrahedra.md)
-GPU-based Projected Tetrahedra is a direct volume rendering method for unstructured tetrahedral data that leverages programmable graphics hardware to achieve interactive frame rates. It extends the classic Projected Tetrahedra algorithm by offloading the geometric classification and decomposition of volume cells to the GPU via vertex shaders, allowing tetrahedral elements to be volume scan-converted within the GPU without pre-processing or additional data structures. Unlike traditional ray-casting approaches which require explicit per-pixel ray-tetrahedron intersection tests, this method treats each tetrahedron as a distinct primitive and rasterizes them using hardware operations, achieving competitive performance with a substantially smaller memory footprint.
+GPU-based Projected Tetrahedra is a direct volume rendering method for unstructured tetrahedral data that achieves interactive rates by treating each tetrahedron as a distinct geometric primitive and rasterizing it using hardware operations. It differs from traditional ray casting by leveraging hardware rasterization and blending to compute pixel color, avoiding explicit per-pixel ray-tetrahedron intersection tests. It builds on [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md) by mapping the classification and decomposition logic into programmable vertex shaders, allowing tetrahedral elements to be volume scan-converted within the GPU without pre-processing or additional data structures.
 
 ### [Texture-Encoded Isosurfacing](7682-texture-encoded-isosurfacing.md)
-Texture-Encoded Isosurfacing is a GPU-based method for extracting isosurfaces from unstructured grids by leveraging 2D texturing hardware to compute intersection points. It builds on [Tetrahedral representation and traversal](4396-tetrahedral-representation-and-traversal.md) by decomposing cells into smaller tetrahedra and projecting their front and back faces to exploit geometric coincidence. Unlike standard projected tetrahedra methods that render triangles, this approach renders fewer faces per tetrahedron by using the tetrahedral decomposition directly, mapping the scalar field values to texture coordinates to determine isosurface intersections via texture sampling.
+Texture-Encoded Isosurfacing is a GPU-based method for extracting isosurfaces from unstructured tetrahedral grids by leveraging 2D texturing hardware to compute intersection points. It solves the problem of efficiently rasterizing implicit surfaces on unstructured data by replacing explicit geometric intersection calculations with texture lookups. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm](940-marching-tetrahedrons-algorithm.md) by avoiding the generation of explicit triangle primitives for the surface; instead, it projects tetrahedra faces and uses a checkerboard texture to encode the scalar field values, allowing the GPU's texture sampling hardware to determine the isosurface location.
+
+### [Parallel Projected Tetrahedra with KD-Tree Partitioning](8767-parallel-projected-tetrahedra-with-kd-tree-partitioning.md)
+This method enables parallel rendering of large unstructured tetrahedral grids by partitioning the data using a kd-tree and applying a sort-last compositing algorithm. It renders unstructured grids natively without sampling into a regular grid, avoiding the oversampling and undersampling issues inherent in grid-based approaches. Unlike other parallel rendering approaches that require continuous cell transfer, this method requires no further cell transfer once distributed; only image data is communicated between processes.
+
+### [Tetrahedral Mesh Connectivity Encoding](8957-tetrahedral-mesh-connectivity-encoding.md)
+A method to encode the connectivity of a tetrahedral mesh segment using opcodes that describe the position of new vertices relative to a growing region bounded by a cut-border, ensuring unique identification of vertices across multi-resolution segments.
 
